<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Porus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 317 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Porus was an Indian king who ruled the land between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jhelum">Jhelum</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chenab">Chenab</a> rivers in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>. In 326 BCE he fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-hydaspes">Battle of the Hydaspes</a> with a large army including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-elephant">war elephants</a>, and was defeated. Impressed by his bearing, Alexander restored him to his kingdom and enlarged it. He was murdered by the Macedonian officer Eudemus around 317 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/porus.jpg" alt="Porus" width="320" height="323" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: User PHGCOM · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Porus_alexander_coin.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His conquest of the Achaemenid Persian Empire included victories at the Granicus (334 BCE), Issus (333 BCE) and Gaugamela (331 BCE), the sieges of Tyre and Gaza (332 BCE), and the defeat of <b>Porus</b> at the Hydaspes (326 BCE), successes in pitched battle and in siege warfare alike.</blockquote><a href="/empire/macedonian-empire">Macedonian Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Its king, called <b>Porus</b> by the Greeks, perhaps from the Vedic clan name Puru, was a rival of Ambhi of the Kingdom of Taxila.</blockquote><a href="/state/paurava-kingdom">Paurava kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/pakistan">Pakistan</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/pakistan">Pakistan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=31,74&amp;year=-327" aria-label="Punjab on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6464 1034 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Porus"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6914 1304h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6914 1304h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=31,74&amp;year=-327">What was here in 327 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Treat me, O Alexander, in a kingly way!&#8221;</p><footer>Arrian, Anabasis, book 5 &#183; tr. E. J. Chinnock, 1884 &#183; asked by the victorious Alexander how he wished to be treated, 326 BCE; Arrian's account &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/46976/pg46976-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 539 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
